We will do this in 5 steps.

STEP 1 — Prepare the AWS Ubuntu VM

First, go to:

AWS Console → EC2 → Instances → your instance → Connect

Use EC2 Instance Connect.

You should get a terminal like:

ubuntu@ip-172-31-12-9:~$

Run these commands one by one:

whoami

Expected:ubuntu

Then:java -version
If Java is NOT installed

Run:
sudo apt update

Then:
sudo apt install -y openjdk-17-jre

Then: java -version

You should see Java 17. Also create the Jenkins working directory

Run: mkdir -p /home/ubuntu/jenkins-agent
Then:chmod 755 /home/ubuntu/jenkins-agent

Stop here. STEP 2 — Create SSH key on Jenkins Controller
Now go to your Jenkins Controller terminal.
You previously showed that your controller is:
jenkins-master-vm

and you can access it through Google Cloud SSH-in-browser.

You should see:

root@jenkins-master-vm:~#

Run:sudo -u jenkins mkdir -p /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh

Then create the key: sudo -u jenkins ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/aws-agent

When it asks for passphrase:
Enter passphrase:
Press Enter.
Then press Enter again.

You now have:/var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/aws-agent - PRIVATE KEY
and: /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/aws-agent.pub - PUBLIC KEY

STEP 3 — Put the PUBLIC key on AWS
Still on the Jenkins Controller, run:
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/aws-agent.pub

You will see one line:
ssh-rsa AAAA.... jenkins@jenkins-master-vm

Copy the whole line.
Now go back to your AWS EC2 terminal.

Run:
nano ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Paste the public key.

Save:

Ctrl + O
Enter
Ctrl + X

Then run:

chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
chown -R ubuntu:ubuntu ~/.ssh
STEP 4 — Test SSH

Now go back to the Jenkins Controller terminal.

Run:

sudo -u jenkins ssh -i /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/aws-agent ubuntu@43.204.144.140

If it asks:

Are you sure you want to continue connecting?

type:

yes
SUCCESS looks like:
Welcome to Ubuntu

and:

ubuntu@ip-172-31-12-9:~$

If you get:

Permission denied (publickey)

stop and send me the screenshot.

Do not continue to Jenkins configuration until this SSH test works.

STEP 5 — Configure Jenkins Credential

Only after Step 4 works.

Go to:

Jenkins → Manage Jenkins → Credentials → System → Global credentials

Create/edit:

Kind:
SSH Username with private key

Username:
ubuntu

Private Key:
Enter directly

For the private key, on the Jenkins Controller run:

sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/.ssh/aws-agent

Copy the complete output and paste it into the Jenkins Key box.

It starts with something like:

-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----

and ends with:

-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----

Leave Passphrase empty because we created the key without one.

Save it as:

ID: aws-agent
STEP 6 — Configure the Jenkins Node

Go to:

Manage Jenkins → Nodes → aws-agent → Configure

Use:

Name:
aws-agent

Number of executors:
1

Remote root directory:
/home/ubuntu/jenkins-agent

Launch method:
Launch agents via SSH

Host:
43.204.144.140

Credentials:
aws-agent

For now:

Host Key Verification Strategy:
Non verifying Verification Strategy

Save.

Then launch the agent.

